{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 42, :comments ["<b>And the fear of Yitzchak was for me.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): He whose fear Yitzchak feared — meaning Elohim — was my helper. And this is the intent of Onkelos." "<b>And there are those</b> who explained that 'the fear of Yitzchak' alludes to the day of the Akeidah, or to the day of the blessing, for there Yitzchak trembled a great trembling when he saw Gehenna beneath him, and at that time he fulfilled his blessing of Yaakov when he said (Genesis 27:33): ״גם ברוך יהיה״. This is what he said: ״כי עתה ריקם שלחתני״ — that is, were it not for that fear which was on my account and for my benefit." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah, 'and the fear of Yitzchak was for me' — in my attribute. And with this he mentioned the three attributes of the patriarchs in order. And likewise he mentioned again in this same order in prayer, against Esav. And what he said first — ״לולי אלהי אבי״ — he said regarding Yitzchak his father. And after it he mentioned Avraham, because a person is obligated in the honor of his father more than the honor of his grandfather. After that he explicitly mentioned 'the fear of Yitzchak,' in the order of the attributes." "<b>And the Ramban</b> wrote concerning <b>and the fear of Yitzchak was for me</b>: By way of the truth, the expression comes according to its plain sense and its meaning, and it is the attribute of Judgment above. And from it Scripture said (Hosea 3:5): ״אחר ישובו בני ישראל ובקשו את ה' אלהיהם ואת דוד מלכם ופחדו אל ה' ואל טובו באחרית הימים״. It means that they will seek the mercy and the attribute of Judgment below, and they will bring the fear of Yitzchak to HaShem and to His goodness mentioned — end of his words." "<b>My affliction and the labor of my hands Elohim saw, and He rebuked you last night.</b> He rebuked you last night for not having killed me. If so, the merit of the labor of his hands saved him from being killed, and the merit of fear of Heaven saved his property — this is what he said: <b>and the fear of Yitzchak was for me, for otherwise you would have sent me away empty.</b> And from here one may learn how great is one who enjoys from his own labor, more than one who fears Heaven."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:53.547360254Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}